# 20. پروگراممېر ياكى AI ئۈچۈن MASTER DEVELOPMENT PROMPT

PROJECT: KARLUK MP3 PRO — ULTIMATE EDITION  
  
Build a complete, production-quality native Android music player named Karluk MP3 Pro.  
  
BRAND:  
Use the exact user-provided Karluk / Zafer logo as the official brand identity. Preserve the original artwork. Prepare appropriate Android launcher/adaptive icon assets and a splash screen. Use a navy-blue and silver-white identity with restrained cyan/blue/violet lighting.  
  
PLATFORM AND STACK:  
Use Kotlin, Jetpack Compose, AndroidX Media3 ExoPlayer, MediaSessionService, Room, DataStore, Hilt, Kotlin Coroutines/Flow, Navigation Compose and Clean Architecture with MVVM. Select compatible stable dependency versions and document them.  
  
LANGUAGES:  
Support Uyghur, Arabic, English and Turkish. Uyghur and Arabic must use correct RTL behavior; English and Turkish must use LTR. All interface strings must be localized resources. Test mixed-direction text, numbers, icons and punctuation.  
  
DESIGN:  
Implement a premium glassmorphism interface inspired by translucent glass controls, frosted panels, soft neumorphic shadows, navy backgrounds, silver borders, blue/cyan/violet lighting, responsive layouts and smooth but battery-conscious animations. Create a consistent design system with tokens for colors, spacing, typography, radius, elevation, blur and motion. Provide reduced-motion and low-performance fallbacks.  
  
SCREENS:  
Splash, onboarding, home dashboard, music library, all songs, albums, artists, folders, now playing, lyrics, playlists, favorites, equalizer, audio effects, search, file manager, playback queue, history, sleep timer, settings, theme studio, storage information and about.  
  
FUNCTIONAL REQUIREMENTS:  
1. Discover local audio through MediaStore and user-selected folders through Storage Access Framework.  
2. Read metadata and album artwork.  
3. Implement actual playback with Media3 ExoPlayer.  
4. Implement queue, shuffle, repeat, seek, previous, next, play/pause and playback speed.  
5. Support background playback through MediaSessionService.  
6. Provide media notification, lock-screen controls, Bluetooth/headset controls and audio-focus handling.  
7. Persist playlists, favorites, history and settings using Room/DataStore.  
8. Implement search, sorting, filtering and multi-select operations.  
9. Implement LRC lyrics synchronization when valid lyric data exists.  
10. Implement equalizer and supported audio effects using Android audio APIs. Detect unsupported features and never display fake success.  
11. Implement sleep timer and mini player.  
12. Provide robust permission-denied, missing-file, unsupported-format, empty-library and playback-error states.  
13. Keep core functionality offline-first; no mandatory account, subscription or cloud upload.  
  
ARCHITECTURE:  
Separate Presentation, Domain, Data, Playback and Android System Integration responsibilities. Use ViewModels and StateFlow for UI state. Playback service/player must be the single source of truth for playback. Keep heavy work off the main thread. Use lifecycle-aware collection, repositories, use cases, database migrations and dependency injection.  
  
SECURITY:  
Request only necessary permissions. Respect Android scoped storage and SAF. Do not access user files without permission. Keep music and user data local by default.  
  
QUALITY:  
Write unit, repository, database, playback, UI navigation, localization and device tests. Test on multiple Android versions and screen sizes. Optimize startup, scrolling, artwork cache, memory, CPU and battery.  
  
DELIVERABLES:  
Provide a complete Android Studio project, source code, debug APK, signed release APK, app icons, splash assets, four-language resources, README, build instructions, test report and release notes.  
  
STRICT DEFINITION OF DONE:  
Do not deliver a static mockup or nonfunctional prototype. Every visible control must perform its stated action. All required screens must be connected. The APK must build, install and run. Any platform limitation must be documented honestly. Do not claim a feature works unless it has been implemented and tested.

